# ---- Firebase (client-side; safe to expose, security comes from Firestore rules) ----
# Firebase console → Project settings → General → Your apps → SDK setup and configuration
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ---- Firebase App Check (abuse protection; optional but recommended) ----
# reCAPTCHA v3 SITE key (the secret key isn't used — attestation is verified by
# Firebase, not by us). google.com/recaptcha/admin → register a v3 site, then
# Firebase console → App Check → Apps → Web → reCAPTCHA v3.
# Leave blank and App Check is a no-op; see README "3. App Check" for the
# ship-key-then-enforce order, which matters.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
# Local dev only — reCAPTCHA can't pass against a production site key from
# localhost. Set to "true", then register the token the SDK logs under
# App Check → Manage debug tokens. NEVER set this in production: a registered
# debug token bypasses attestation completely. (A production build now IGNORES
# this and logs an error if it's set, but keep it out of prod regardless.)
NEXT_PUBLIC_APPCHECK_DEBUG=
# Server-side App Check enforcement on the paid routes (/api/analyze,
# /api/speech). Those routes verify the X-Firebase-AppCheck header the client
# sends, so a valid ID token alone can't drive them from a script. Leave
# unset/"false" to SOFT-FAIL: an unattested request is LOGGED but still served,
# so browsers on a bundle predating the header aren't locked out. Once the
# server logs ([app-check] unattested ...) fall to ~zero, set to "true" to
# hard-reject unattested requests with a 403. Same ship-then-enforce order as
# the Firestore rollout above.
APPCHECK_ENFORCE=

# ---- Analysis pipeline (server-side only; never exposed to the browser) ----
# assemblyai.com → free tier includes $50 credit, no card required
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=
# aistudio.google.com → Get API key
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ---- Felix's voice (server-side only; never exposed to the browser) ----
# fish.audio → API keys. The free developer tier (model "s2.1-pro-free") is
# plenty: a take is thirty to sixty words, synthesized once per report and
# cached on the session. Unset, /api/voice answers 503 and the "Hear Felix's
# feedback" button says so quietly; the written take stays. Felix's take
# itself (the words) comes from GEMINI_API_KEY above, via /api/felix.
FISH_AUDIO_API_KEY=
# The voice Felix speaks in: the "reference_id" from a voice's page on
# fish.audio's library. Unset = Fish Audio's stock voice, which is fine for
# trying it, and not a fox.
FISH_AUDIO_VOICE_ID=
# Model header. Defaults to s2.1-pro-free; s2.1-pro / s2-pro / s1 on a paid
# plan. Also read by scripts/felix-voice-sample.mjs, which writes the
# landing page's static /felix-hello.mp3 once so visitors hear him for free.
FISH_AUDIO_MODEL=

# ---- Stripe (server-side; subscriptions + billing) ----
# Prefer a RESTRICTED key (rk_...) over the account secret key (sk_...):
# dashboard.stripe.com → Developers → API keys → Create restricted key.
# This app needs write on Customers, Checkout Sessions, Customer portal
# (the dashboard's label for Billing Portal Sessions), and Subscriptions;
# read on Invoices. Nothing else — a leaked restricted key then can't move
# money or read the rest of the account.
# Use a separate key per environment (test / production).
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Developers → Webhooks → add endpoint (/api/stripe/webhook) → Signing secret
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Products → the three recurring Price IDs (price_...).
# These live on three separate products — "Elovox Premium (Weekly/Monthly/
# Yearly)". Nothing in the code cares: checkout and the webhook key off the
# Price ID alone (see cycleForPriceId in lib/pricing.ts). The only thing the
# split affects is the Customer Portal, which must list all three products
# for plan switching to work.
# Checkout passes trial_period_days from lib/pricing.ts (weekly: none,
# monthly 7, annual 7). The Prices themselves must carry NO trial of their
# own (recurring.trial_period_days = null) — verified on the configured
# Prices, and matching lib/pricing.ts and STRIPE-GO-LIVE.md.
#
# This matters more than it looks: "no trial" is expressed by OMITTING
# trial_period_days (Stripe rejects a zero-day trial), so a trial set on the
# Price itself would be inherited exactly in the two cases we omit it — the
# "pay today, skip the trial" option, and the once-per-customer guard that
# stops someone cancelling and re-subscribing for another free week. Both
# would silently stop working. Checkout logs an error if it ever sees one.
STRIPE_PRICE_WEEKLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_ANNUAL=

# ---- Firebase Admin (server-side; the ONLY writer of profile/plan) ----
# Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new
# private key. Paste the whole JSON on one line (or base64-encode it).
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=

# Public base URL, used to build Stripe return URLs. Optional: falls back
# to the request's own origin. e.g. https://elovox.app
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# ---- Operator access ----
# Comma-separated emails allowed to see /admin (the stats dashboard).
# Matched against the VERIFIED email on the caller's Firebase ID token.
# Unset/empty = nobody has access and /admin 404s, which is the safe default.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# ---- Login throttling (lib/loginGuard.ts, /api/auth/login) ----
# Salt for the SHA-256 keys of the login-attempt ledger. Any long random
# string. Optional but strongly recommended: without it the hashes are still
# irreversible, but they are enumerable against a word list by anyone who
# obtains a database dump. Rotating it resets every in-flight lockout.
LOGIN_HASH_SALT=

# ---- Outbound mail (lib/email/) ----
# Everything Elovox sends itself goes through Resend. Verification and
# user-requested password resets are still sent by Firebase Auth from its own
# templates; everything else — the lockout notice, welcome, billing, weekly
# progress, the tips list — is lib/email/messages.ts.
#
# Unset = nothing sends. Every path degrades quietly and says so in the log;
# nothing breaks, and a lockout still applies whether or not it can be mailed.
# See docs/EMAIL.md for the full setup, including the DNS records.
#
# REQUIRED
# A Resend API key (https://resend.com). Prefer a SENDING-ONLY key over full
# access: Resend -> API Keys -> Create -> permission "Sending access". The
# admin console's domain panel needs "Full access" to read domain status; it
# degrades to "unknown" on a sending-only key, which is the right trade.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# The From address. MUST be on a domain verified in Resend. A display name is
# worth having ("Elovox <hello@elovox.app>"); a bare address is accepted and
# gets wrapped in the service name automatically.
MAIL_FROM=
# Where replies land. Defaults to LEGAL.emails.support. Point it at a mailbox a
# person actually reads — a From with no working Reply-To is scored against
# the sending domain, and a user replying with a real problem gets a bounce.
MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# STRONGLY RECOMMENDED
# Resend -> Webhooks -> add endpoint https://<your-domain>/api/resend/webhook,
# then copy the signing secret (whsec_...). WITHOUT THIS, bounce and spam
# complaints are never recorded and the app keeps mailing dead addresses,
# which is the fastest way to lose a sending domain's reputation. The webhook
# route refuses every request while this is unset rather than accepting
# unsigned ones — an unverified endpoint that writes to the suppression list
# would let anyone block anyone's password-reset email.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# OPTIONAL
# Signs the unsubscribe links. Falls back to a hash of FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT
# when unset, so links work out of the box — but then rotating the service
# account breaks every link in every email already delivered. Set it to any
# long random string and that stops being true.
EMAIL_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Resend -> Audiences -> create one for the tips list, then paste its id.
# Unset = contacts aren't mirrored and Broadcasts are unavailable; every
# transactional path is unaffected.
RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=

# ---- Cron ----
# Shared secret for the two scheduled routes (/api/cron/purge-ops and
# /api/cron/email), sent by Vercel Cron as "Authorization: Bearer <value>".
# REQUIRED in production — both routes refuse to run without it rather than
# falling open, and the email one SENDS MAIL, so there is no "harmless
# anyway" argument for an anonymous fallback.
CRON_SECRET=

# ---- Development escape hatches (compiled out of production builds) ----
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FORCE_PLAN=premium shows every gated surface without a Stripe
# subscription. Ignored when NODE_ENV=production, so it cannot be switched on
# by accident in a deployed build — but it inlines at BUILD time, so don't set
# it in a production environment either.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FORCE_PLAN=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_APPCHECK_DEBUG=<token> registers a debug App Check token.
# Refused outright in a production build (see lib/appCheck.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_APPCHECK_DEBUG=

# APPCHECK_ENFORCE=true makes /api/analyze and /api/speech REJECT unattested
# callers instead of logging and allowing them. Turn this on once the
# soft-fail log volume has fallen to ~zero.
APPCHECK_ENFORCE=
